from pathlib import Path
p=Path('manuscript/main.tex');s=p.read_text()
def replace(old,new):
 global s
 assert s.count(old)==1,(old,s.count(old))
 s=s.replace(old,new)
replace('A prevalence-conditioned null and competitive mapping would be needed to interpret temporal persistence beyond repeated DNA detection.',r'''An exploratory null that permuted expedition labels within site and soil position, holding family occupancy and co-occurrence fixed, gave a mean all-five frequency of 82.7\% (central 95\% null range 81.1--84.2\%; 499 permutations). The observed frequency was 3.36 percentage points lower (lower-tail permutation $p = 0.002$). Repeated detection is therefore interpreted relative to prevalence and sampling structure; expedition-associated assay differences, mapping ambiguity and persistent DNA remain possible contributors.''')
replace('Harmonised gene prediction would further distinguish these cases.',r'''In an exploratory caller substitution, applying the original MAG-caller configuration to the same culture assemblies reduced the dark no-CDS count from 344 to 235 of 735 (32.0\%). Of the original 344, 118 gained an overlapping call and nine other loci lost one; 116 gains had compatible orientation and first-base phase. The no-CDS fraction fell in 11 genome pairs and was unchanged in 11. Annotated calls changed from 607 to 498 of 67{,}802. Among complete dark calls of at least 100 residues, the count fell from 25 to 17 of 59. For the 42 DNA-supported calls belonging to admitted families, both callers left 18 without overlap, with one gain and one loss. These results locate caller sensitivity in the broader call set; the small recurrent-family subset retains the same aggregate discrepancy. Coding status remains unresolved by overlap and first-base phase alone.''')
replace('so presence-recall in those samples measures overlap with the reference universe.',r'''so presence-recall in those samples measures overlap with the reference universe.
An exploratory comparison over a fixed common set of 8{,}860 orthologues, including zero ties, gave a mean paired rank correlation of 0.8431. Permuting sample identities within expedition and soil position gave a mean of 0.8245, a paired advantage of 0.0185. Restricting exchanges further to site left 146 exchangeable samples, with a paired mean of 0.8431 against 0.8408, an advantage of 0.0023 (upper-tail permutation $p = 0.0002$ for each design). Thus the profiles share a large reference-wide rank pattern and retain a small sample-specific component under site conditioning. This mean over a fixed universe is a different estimand from the median shared-orthologue correlation above. Abundance-threshold sensitivity is reported in Supplementary Note S2.''')
replace('representatives at 30\\% identity.',r'''representatives at 30\% identity.
The exploratory expedition-label null used the 106{,}042-family by 551-sample binary detection matrix. Within each of 159 site-by-soil-position strata, we permuted expedition labels and applied the same global permutation to every family, preserving all family occupancies, co-occurrence patterns and stratum-specific expedition sample counts. Of these strata, 121 contained multiple expedition labels (509 samples). We used 499 permutations (seed 20261015), reporting the all-five frequency, central null quantiles and tail proportions with a plus-one correction. Six fixed occupancy bins were retained as descriptive sensitivities. Label exchangeability within strata is an assumption; sequencing and mapping differences associated with expedition remain possible explanations.''')
# Add caller method at end of culture subsection, immediately before next heading.
a=s.index('\\subsection*{Cultured-genome comparison}')
b=s.index('\\subsection*{',a+len('\\subsection*{'))
s=s[:b]+r'''For exploratory caller substitution, we called the 22 culture assemblies with Pyrodigal v3.7.1 in metagenomic mode (\texttt{-p meta --no-stop-codon}), matching the original MAG-caller configuration while holding nucleotide alignments and eligible MAG calls fixed. The locus-overlap classification was applied to each caller's GFF at 50\% and 90\% of aligned target span; at 50\%, all 68{,}537 Bakta classifications reproduced the retained locus audit. Subsets used the original complete-call flag, exact protein length of at least 100 residues, and admitted-family membership. We retained per-genome-pair transitions and treated calls as descriptive observations. The 90\% criterion gave dark no-CDS counts of 400/735 with Bakta and 281/735 with Pyrodigal; the admitted-family subset remained 18/42 with each caller. The substitution assesses annotation sensitivity at DNA-supported loci; full codon correspondence and translation require additional evidence.

'''+s[b:]
replace('gap, not prediction error.',r'''gap, not prediction error.
For the exploratory paired-profile null, both matrices were normalised within sample and ranks were computed over their fixed intersection of 8{,}860 orthologue identifiers, including zero ties. We compared the mean matched-sample Spearman correlation with 4{,}999 permutations of PICRUSt sample labels within expedition and soil position (15 blocks, 448 samples), then within expedition, position and site (66 blocks with at least two samples, 146 samples). Only exchangeable samples contributed to each statistic; singleton strata were excluded. Random permutations could retain fixed points. The random generator used seed 20261016 across the two designs; tail proportions used a plus-one correction. A fixed threshold grid of $0$, $10^{-6}$, $10^{-5}$, $10^{-4}$ and $10^{-3}$ was applied to each normalised profile over the union of identifiers. These post hoc tests describe profile agreement and threshold dependence conditional on recovered genomes and prediction references.''')
replace('compatible-solute synthesis panel (Supplementary Table~S3) against the',r'''compatible-solute synthesis panel against the''')
replace('orthologue table, prevalences being fractions of the 5{,}438 representatives.',r'''orthologue table, prevalences being fractions of the 5{,}438 representatives. The solute panel contains K00697, K01087, K16055, K13057, K06044, K01236, K06720, K10674, K03692, K05978, K05947, K18818 and K07026. Trehalose carriage accepts K00697 or K16055 or K13057 or the pair K06044/K01236; ectoine uses K06720, glucosylglycerol K03692, and mannosylglycerate either K05947 or K18818. K10674 is reported separately as a hydroxyectoine-associated marker. Strict-route sensitivities require K16055 or both K00697/K01087 for trehalose, both K03692/K05978 for glucosylglycerol, and K18818 or both K05947/K07026 for mannosylglycerate.''')
replace('\\section*{Data and code availability}',r'''\section*{Supplementary Note S2: functional-profile threshold sensitivity}

The paired functional profiles were compared at five fixed within-sample orthologue-fraction thresholds. Median Jaccard similarity was 0.634 at abundance greater than zero, 0.697 at $10^{-6}$, 0.696 at $10^{-5}$ and 0.683 at $10^{-4}$; all 448 samples retained genome-encoded orthologues at these thresholds. Median numbers retained in the genome and PICRUSt profiles were, respectively, 8{,}666.5 and 10{,}543 at zero, 7{,}270.5 and 6{,}547.5 at $10^{-6}$, 5{,}268 and 4{,}932.5 at $10^{-5}$, and 2{,}572.5 and 2{,}507.5 at $10^{-4}$. At $10^{-3}$, 446 samples retained no genome-encoded orthologues, making recall undefined for those samples; the median Jaccard was zero. The two profiles have different constructions, so a shared relative-abundance threshold can have different detection implications. These sensitivities describe reference overlap under fixed rules. Incomplete genome recovery limits the interpretation of orthologues present only in predictions.

\section*{Data and code availability}''')
